PLACES=[
N('johnson-hall-johnstown','Johnson Hall','Johnstown','mohawk','landmark','standing',43.01626,-74.38333,'exact','public','drive','','1763',
  'Sir William Johnson, the Crown\'s Superintendent of Indian Affairs, moved here from Old Fort Johnson in 1763 and lived here until his death in 1774. The house is timber, sided with planks cut to look like stone; Johnson also built two stone blockhouses for defence and storage. His Loyalist son John fled to Canada, the state seized and sold the house in 1779, and it stayed a private home until the state bought it in 1906.',
  'The 1763 house, restored in the early 1900s and again in the 1950s to strip later additions, and the West Stonehouse, the one original blockhouse (the East Stonehouse is a rebuilding, per Wikipedia). Guided tours of the house only; the rooms are furnished for the period, but the original contents were sold after the Revolution, per the state parks page.',
  'The house a colonial empire was run from, built of wood made to pass for stone.',
  'New York State Parks, Hall Avenue. Its page (read November 18, 2026): the grounds are open sunrise to sunset; the 2026 season ran May 23 to October 11, with guided tours of the hall on the hour Wednesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. (last at 3) and Sunday 1 to 4 p.m.; children 12 and under free, groups $6 a head by reservation; adult fee not read. Phone (518) 762-8712.',
  [W('Johnson Hall State Historic Site'),JH],th=[43.01573,-74.38329],thn='Visitor lot below the hall (seen on the aerial)',brick='no'),
N('fulton-county-courthouse-johnstown','Fulton County Courthouse (old Tryon County Courthouse)','Johnstown','mohawk','civic','standing',43.00667,-74.37556,'site','view','drive','','1772–1773',
  'Built in 1772 and 1773 as the courthouse of Tryon County, with £500 toward it from Sir William Johnson. It served Tryon and then Montgomery County until the county seat moved to Fonda in 1836, passed into private hands, and became the courthouse of the new Fulton County in 1838. Wikipedia calls it the oldest courthouse in New York State and one of the oldest in the country still used as one.',
  'A Georgian brick building, five bays by three, with a bellcast gable roof and a cupola of the late 18th or early 19th century, at North William and West Main Streets. Still a working courthouse; interior not checked.',
  'New York\'s oldest courthouse, still hearing cases.',
  'Fulton County; a working court, so see it from the street outside court business. Street parking in downtown Johnstown.',
  [W('Fulton County Courthouse (New York)')],brick='yes'),
N('tryon-county-jail-johnstown','Tryon County Jail (Fort Johnstown)','Johnstown','mohawk','fort','standing',43.00308,-74.37168,'site','view','drive','forts','1772–1773',
  'The stone jail of Tryon County, built in 1772 and 1773 on the village\'s highest ground. In the Revolution it was palisaded with four lookout towers and became Fort Johnstown, inspected by Lafayette in 1778 and the base of Col. Marinus Willett\'s 400 militia in 1781; Washington visited in 1783. It stayed a jail until the 1970s.',
  'The original five-bay stone block with a large brick wing and a smaller brick wing of about 1890 added to make an L, at Perry and Montgomery Streets; in the 2008 Wikipedia photograph the brick wings are painted red and the stone block is bare. It now holds county offices (Wikipedia); interior not checked.',
  'A colonial jail that became a frontier fort.',
  'Fulton County offices; see it from the street. Street parking.',
  [W('Fulton County Jail (Johnstown, New York)')],brick='no'),
N('st-johns-church-johnstown','St. John\'s Episcopal Church and Sir William Johnson\'s grave','Johnstown','mohawk','burial','standing',43.00683,-74.37318,'exact','view','drive','cemeteries','1837 (parish 1760)',
  'Sir William Johnson founded the parish; its first church of 1760, which the state marker calls the first Church of England north of the Mohawk, stood at West State and West Green Streets. The second church burned in 1836, and the present stone Gothic church was finished on this site in 1837 and much renovated in 1911. Johnson\'s grave, marked as such, is in the churchyard.',
  'The 1837 church of random ashlar stone with its stone entrance and bell tower, and in the churchyard the grave marker of Sir William Johnson (1715–1774), reached from Church Street west of Market Street (HMdb). Interior not checked.',
  'The grave of the man who founded the town.',
  'A working parish, 1 North Market Street; the churchyard grave can be seen from Church Street. Interior hours not checked.',
  [W('St. John\'s Episcopal Church (Johnstown, New York)'),L('HMdb: Grave of Sir William Johnson','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=54281'),L('HMdb: Site of First St John\'s Church','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=50747')],brick='no'),
N('johnstown-colonial-cemetery-drumm-house','Johnstown Colonial Cemetery and the Drumm House','Johnstown','mohawk','burial','standing',43.00801,-74.3728,'site','public','walk','cemeteries','1763; cemetery 1766',
  'The town burying ground, established in 1766 and on the National Register since 1998; the 1932 state marker says many of Johnstown\'s colonial and Revolutionary notables lie here. Beside it on West Green Street is the Drumm House, which the county tourism page calls one of the oldest dwellings in Johnstown, the one survivor of six like it Johnson built for his staff, and probably the first home of Edward Wall, master of the free school of 1763.',
  'The old stones of the cemetery at West Green and North Market Streets, and the small Drumm House at West Green and West State Streets. Whether the house opens and when was not checked (the historical society\'s site would not open).',
  '',
  'City of Johnstown cemetery; the Drumm House belongs to the Johnstown historical society, contact through the Johnstown Historian (518) 762-2777. Hours not checked. Street parking.',
  [W('Johnstown Colonial Cemetery'),L('HMdb: Colonial Cemetery marker','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=5686'),L44('The Drumm House','https://www.44lakes.com/things_to_do/the-drumm-house/')],brick='?'),
N('johnstown-battlefield','Battle of Johnstown site','Johnstown','mohawk','fort','marker',43.02093,-74.3816,'area','public','drive','forts','1781',
  'On October 25, 1781, six days after Yorktown, militia under Col. Marinus Willett met a British and Loyalist force under Maj. John Ross and Capt. Walter Butler south and west of the markers and drove it off the field at dusk. Butler was killed a few days later at West Canada Creek in the pursuit.',
  'A 1975 county marker on Johnson Avenue (County Route 115) and a boulder with a bronze tablet of the Johnstown D.A.R. near the O\'Neil Avenue corner. The tourism page dates the boulder to 1909; the tablet itself, as HMdb transcribes it, reads August 31, 1901. Open fields; nothing of the battle to see.',
  'The last fight of the Revolution in the north, after Yorktown.',
  'Roadside markers on Johnson Avenue just north of Johnson Hall. Land beyond the road not checked; stay on the roadside.',
  [L44('Site of the Battle of Johnstown','https://www.44lakes.com/things-to-do/site-of-the-battle-of-johnstown-october-25-1781'),L('HMdb: Battle of Johnstown (1975)','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=58940'),L('HMdb: D.A.R. tablet','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=59006')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('knox-mansion-johnstown','Knox Mansion','Johnstown','mohawk','landmark','standing',42.99849,-74.37449,'exact','ask','drive','','1898 (or 1889); 1908',
  'Charles and Rose Knox, who made Knox Gelatine in Johnstown, built this 42-room Classical Revival house, with an addition of 1908. Wikipedia says 1898, the Haunted History Trail page 1889. It was sold to Rose Knox\'s doctor after her death in 1950, decayed and lost most of its contents, and was renovated after 1993; it has been run as a museum, bed and breakfast and home.',
  'An elevator, a grand staircase and a fireplace said to come from an Italian castle (Wikipedia); interior not checked. Visits by advance reservation only (Haunted History Trail page).',
  'A gelatine fortune\'s 42 rooms.',
  'Private; 104 West Second Avenue. By advance reservation only, phone (518) 752-8231 (Haunted History Trail page, read November 18, 2026).',
  [W('Knox Mansion'),L('Haunted History Trail of New York State: The Knox Mansion','https://hauntedhistorytrail.com/explore/the-knox-mansion')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('glove-theatre-gloversville','Glove Theatre','Gloversville','mohawk','amuse','standing',43.05147,-74.34436,'exact','public','drive','','1914',
  'A movie, vaudeville and civic theatre by the Utica architect Linn Kinne, opened on October 9, 1914, and by the 1950s, with 800 seats, the largest movie house in Fulton County. It decayed from the 1970s and in 1995 was close to coming down for a parking lot, when a community group bought it for $1. It reopened in 1997 and is run by volunteers.',
  'The Classical Revival front of pale brick, with its marquee, on North Main Street between City Hall and the Kasson Block. Inside, the restored ceiling (the theatre\'s own history page); the auditorium can be seen at its shows. Interior not otherwise checked.',
  'Bought for a dollar the year it was to become a parking lot.',
  'Nonprofit theatre, 42 North Main Street; open for performances and events. Street parking downtown.',
  [W('Glove Theatre'),L('The Glove Theatre: history','https://www.theglovetheatre.com/history')],brick='no'),
N('gloversville-free-library','Gloversville Free Library (Carnegie library)','Gloversville','mohawk','civic','standing',43.05057,-74.34294,'exact','public','drive','','1904',
  'A Beaux-Arts library by Albert Randolph Ross of New York, built in 1904 with $50,000 from Andrew Carnegie, whose name is cut over the entrance. A domed entrance hall with the stairs, a central stack and two wings set at 45 degrees. It reopened after renovation on November 5, 2018.',
  'The domed entrance hall and the angled wings; open to the public as the Gloversville Public Library.',
  '',
  'Gloversville Public Library, 58 East Fulton Street. Hours not checked (its page said only that Mondays were curbside service, with no year).',
  [W('Gloversville Free Library')],brick='no'),
N('gloversville-armory','Gloversville Armory','Gloversville','mohawk','civic','standing',43.0499,-74.3405,'exact','view','drive','','1903–1904',
  'A National Guard armory built in 1903 and 1904 to a design by State Architect George L. Heins, of brick and red sandstone and meant to recall a medieval castle. It has a two-story administration building, a drill shed and three towers, the tallest a five-story octagon.',
  'The castle front with its octagonal tower, square tower and crenellated tower, on Washington Street. Still used by the New York Army National Guard (Wikipedia); no public access found.',
  '',
  'New York Army National Guard, 87 Washington Street; see it from the street.',
  [W('Gloversville Armory')],brick='yes'),
N('downtown-gloversville','Downtown Gloversville (the Four Corners)','Gloversville','mohawk','town','standing',43.05083,-74.34528,'area','public','walk','','before 1850–1931',
  'The commercial heart of the glove-making city, grown round the four corners of Main and Fulton Streets; 78 buildings were listed together on the National Register in 1985. Most went up between 1870 and 1900.',
  'Three-story Italianate brick blocks with bracketed cornices, and Neoclassical, Romanesque, Second Empire and Art Deco buildings among them (Wikipedia), on North and South Main, East and West Fulton, Church, Fremont, Spring and Prospect Streets. The pin is the district\'s Register point.',
  '',
  'Public streets; street parking.',
  [W('Downtown Gloversville Historic District')],brick='?'),
N('fulton-county-museum-gloversville','Fulton County Museum (Kingsboro school)','Gloversville','mohawk','inst','standing',43.06593,-74.33631,'exact','public','drive','','1900 (academy 1831)',
  'The Fulton County Historical Society\'s museum, in the Kingsboro school of 1900, built where Rev. Elisha Yale\'s Kingsboro Academy of 1831 stood. The school closed when a new one opened in 1972, and the museum opened in March 1973.',
  'Exhibits on the county\'s glove and leather trade, weaving, the FJ&G Railroad, schools and the military, and the county sports hall of fame (county tourism page). Building interior beyond that not checked.',
  'The glove and leather story of the glove city.',
  'Fulton County Historical Society, 237 Kingsboro Avenue, (518) 725-2203. Hours not checked (the society\'s page returned no text).',
  [L44('Fulton County Historical Society & Museum','https://www.44lakes.com/things-to-do/fulton-county-historical-society-museum/')],brick='?'),
N('rice-homestead-mayfield','Rice Homestead (Oliver Rice House)','Mayfield','mohawk','landmark','standing',43.10007,-74.27735,'exact','public','drive','','about 1790; about 1810',
  'Oliver Rice, who served under Washington in the Revolution, put up a small wing about 1790 (the tourism pages call it the homestead\'s log cabin); the main center-chimney frame house followed about 1810, and the old wing was moved and joined to it before 1847. His descendants lived here until the 1990s. The Mayfield Historical Society now keeps it as a museum.',
  'The 1810 Federal farmhouse with its older wing and a 19th-century brick smokehouse, with Rice family and Mayfield things inside; a 1932 state marker on Riceville Road.',
  '',
  'Mayfield Historical Society, Riceville Road. Open Wednesdays and Saturdays noon to 4 p.m. from mid-June to Labor Day, and by appointment, (518) 332-0538 (mohawkvalleymuseums.us and visitsacandaga.com, read November 18, 2026).',
  [W('Oliver Rice House'),L('Mohawk Valley Museums: Mayfield Historical Society at the Rice Homestead','https://mohawkvalleymuseums.us/museums/mayfield-historical-society/'),L('Visit Sacandaga: The Rice Homestead','https://www.visitsacandaga.com/?p=7077'),L('HMdb: Rice Homestead marker','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=68555')],brick='some'),
N('log-cabin-church-mayfield','Log Cabin Church','Mayfield','mohawk','civic','standing',43.0429,-74.29426,'exact','view','drive','','1937',
  'A non-denominational church built in 1937 of white poplar logs laid horizontally, 26 by 18 feet, in the hamlet of Progress. A privy and a parish hall stand on the lot. It was listed on the National Register in 1999.',
  'The small poplar-log church at 413 Progress Road. Whether it still holds services, and the interior, not checked.',
  '',
  'Private church grounds; see it from Progress Road.',
  [W('Log Cabin Church')],brick='no'),
N('hotel-broadalbin','Hotel Broadalbin','Broadalbin','mohawk','hotel','standing',43.05699,-74.19939,'exact','view','drive','hotels','1854; 1881',
  'A two-story brick block built in 1854 as a glove store, turned into a hotel in 1881 with a frame wing and a two-story hipped verandah. Wikipedia says it was renovated and reopened as a full-service hotel in July 2019; a commercial real-estate listing for 59 West Main Street came up in a search in November 2026, so whether it is open now was not checked.',
  'The brick glove-store block and the 1881 clapboard wing with the long verandah on West Main Street. Interior not checked.',
  'A glove store that became the village hotel.',
  '59 West Main Street; see it from the street. Whether the hotel is trading was not checked.',
  [W('Hotel Broadalbin')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('fish-house-drowned','Fish House and the drowned Sacandaga crossroads','Northampton','adk','town','water',43.14627,-74.12645,'area','public','drive','drowned','1762–1930',
  'Sir William Johnson built a fishing lodge here in 1762, and the hamlet took its name from it; Tories and Indians burned the lodge in 1781. In 1818 the state built a 380-foot double-barrel covered bridge over the Sacandaga, which stood until 1930. That year the Sacandaga Reservoir flooded the centre of Fish House, where five roads met among hotels, homes and stores.',
  'State markers of 1932 for the lodge site and the covered bridge, and a 2019 marker at South Shore Road and Fish House Road for the old road that runs into the water toward the drowned centre. Nothing of the bridge or lodge is said to be left.',
  'A hamlet named for Sir William\'s fishing lodge, half under the reservoir.',
  'Roadside markers at South Shore Road (County Route 110) and Fish House Road. The lake shore is mostly private; stay on the road.',
  [L('HMdb: "Fish House" (lodge site)','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=45607'),L('HMdb: Covered Bridge','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=44404'),L('HMdb: Old Fish House road (2019)','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=190495')],brick='no'),
N('shew-house-fish-house','Godfrey Shew House','Northampton','adk','landmark','standing',43.14643,-74.12346,'site','view','drive','','1784',
  'Godfrey Shew, Fish House\'s first settler under Sir William Johnson in 1762, was taken to Canada with his sons John, Stephen and Jacob in a Tory and Indian raid on June 3, 1778. On their return they built this house in 1784, after the first had been destroyed in the raids. It is a two-story, five-bay Federal frame house with a one-story wing.',
  'The 1784 house with its four-column Tuscan entrance porch and a carriage house of about 1885, and the 1939 state marker in front. Private home; interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private; see it from South Shore Road (County Route 110), the last house in the hamlet before the Saratoga County line.',
  [W('Godfrey Shew House'),L('HMdb: Shew House','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=174867'),L('HMdb: Godfrey Shew','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=46497'),L('Visit Sacandaga: Shew House','https://www.visitsacandaga.com/?p=9848')],brick='?'),
N('caroga-museum','Caroga Historical Museum','Caroga','adk','inst','standing',43.14679,-74.49039,'site','public','drive','amusements','1870; 1905',
  'An outdoor museum of the Caroga Historical Association, set up in 1981 on the Arnst family homestead on London Bridge Road. The farmhouse had been a tanner\'s house, moved from near the old tannery and put back together here; the cobbler\'s shop was Eune Arnst\'s own.',
  'A 1905 farmhouse, an 1870 cobbler\'s shop, a general store and a pegged barn about 130 years old, with displays on tanning and lumbering and a large collection from Sherman\'s Amusement Park (county tourism page).',
  '',
  'Caroga Historical Association, 145 London Bridge Road (County Highway 111). Open July and August, Thursday to Sunday 1 to 4 p.m. (county tourism page, read November 18, 2026; the museum\'s own page not checked for hours).',
  [L44('Caroga Historical Association and Museum','https://www.44lakes.com/things_to_do/caroga-historical-association-and-museum/'),L('Caroga Museum: History of the Museum','https://www.carogamuseum.org/?p=583')],brick='?'),
N('northville-historic-district','Northville village','Northville','adk','town','standing',43.22591,-74.17192,'area','public','walk','','about 1819–1933',
  'The village on its peninsula in the Sacandaga, listed in 2014 as a district of 176 buildings from about 1819 to 1933 along South Main, Bridge and Division Streets.',
  'The Ray Hubbell House (about 1880), St. Francis of Assisi church (1896, 1922), the Chequer & Kested blacksmith shop (about 1870), the Mosher Brothers store (1915), the Baptist church (1903), the Yates House (about 1840), the Methodist church (1871) and the Star Theatre (about 1850–70), per Wikipedia. Interiors not checked.',
  '',
  'Public streets; street parking.',
  [W('Northville Historic District (New York)')],brick='?'),
]
